# The D diagnostic (council rounds 103 to 106): the sentence is not adopted; the obligation split does not recur when D is read alone

**In one line:** fresh readers of both families, reading only the due-day mark, hold D on all 12 obligation yearly figures whose day
code set. That includes the 8 the Codex readers had failed in the full readings, and it holds under the unchanged brief as under
the clarified one. The sentence changes no mark in either family, and the prewritten rule does not adopt it.

- One draw per reader.
- Future gate reports note it as a reader discrepancy (council round 107), not as a proven artefact.

## The marks (`score-output.txt`; frozen expectations in `expected.json`, hashes in `sha256.txt`)

| Group | Expected | Codex, unchanged | Codex, clarified | Claude, unchanged | Claude, clarified | Earlier full-form readers, Codex / Claude |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1-8 obligation yearly figures, code's day, split before | holds | 8 holds | 8 holds | 8 holds | 8 holds | 8 fails / 8 holds |
| 9-12 the same class, agreed before | holds | 4 holds | 4 holds | 4 holds | 4 holds | holds / holds |
| 13-15 fail controls (a day's price, a 12-month window, a quarter) | fails | 3 fails | 3 fails | 3 fails | 3 fails | fails / fails |
| 17-18 agreed acquisitions shown with an 18-month check day | fails | 2 fails | 2 fails | **2 holds** | **2 holds** | fails / fails |
| 19-22 stated days | holds | 4 holds | 4 holds | 4 holds | 4 holds | holds / holds |
| 23 constructed: the stated filing day shown | holds | holds | holds | holds | holds | (new) |
| 24 constructed: a stated filing day, code's day shown | fails | fails | fails | fails | fails | (new) |
| 16 uncounted (a rolling window's last day) | n/a | fails | fails | fails | fails | fails / fails |

**The rule's result: NOT ADOPTED.**

- Under the sentence, Claude fails 3 of the 5 fail controls, where 4 are needed: it holds the two acquisitions.
- It does the same under the unchanged brief, so the sentence did not cause it.
- Every other check passes.
- No control moved between the two briefs, in either family.

## What it shows, and what it does not

- **A reader discrepancy, not a proven cause** (council round 107):
  - In one draw, a fresh Codex reader under the unchanged brief applies D414 to all 12 class items, including 8 whose earlier
    full-form Codex reading failed. It also keeps a stated filing day (item 23) and fails code's day against it (item 24).
  - That suggests a context or reader effect of the full-form reading.
  - It does not identify the cause, and it does not show that all 128 earlier marks would change: one draw per reader, 8 of the 23
    class topics.
  - The two countings bracket the unresolved rulings. They do not decide which reading is correct.
- **Claude reading D alone departs from both families' full-form reading on agreed acquisitions** (items 17 and 18). So a D-only
  pass is not simply more accurate; each family moves on a different class.
- **It gives no overall reader error rate,** and it does not prove what caused the earlier split.


